{"text": " Kenya loses 30% to 40% of its food to post-Havest losses every year. Amaze farmer in Eldoret sells at 28 Kenyan shillings per kilo in March, when the price is at its seasonal low, then watches the price hit 55 Kenyan shillings in August. An agri tech startup with a price forecasting model helped 3,200 farmers in the Rift Valley time their sales better in 2023. Average income improvement 18,000 Kenyan shillings per household per season. One AI model built by one data analyst, 3,200 families. In this lesson, the five highest value AI applications in East African agriculture and the specific problems each one solves, where AI works reliably in agricultural contexts, where it fails, and why East Africa is a higher opportunity AI agriculture market than most Western contexts due to the combination of mobile connectivity, MPSA infrastructure, and data scarcity that AI can help address. AI adds value in agriculture when it can process data faster or at greater scale than a human expert and when that faster processing changes a decision. The five highest value agricultural AI applications in East Africa are one crop yield prediction using weather data, soil sensors, satellite imagery, and historical yield records to forecast the output of a specific farm or cooperative plot weeks before harvest. This changes planting decisions, input purchasing, and storage preparation. The single most important rule for agricultural AI in East Africa is design for the interface constraints of the target user first. If your AI output requires a web browser and reliable internet, it will never reach a smallholder farmer in Meru or a Fisher in Kisumu. WhatsApp and SMS are the only universal delivery channels. Any AI system that cannot deliver its recommendations through one of these two channels cannot serve the majority of the agricultural workforce. Remember this, agricultural AI that cannot reach the farmer on the device they already have and the network they already use is a laboratory exercise, not a deployed product. Always design for WhatsApp and SMS delivery before anything else. Apply what you learn in this lesson and you will see results immediately. Your skills bear fruit.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 4.98, "text": " Kenya loses 30% to 40% of its food to post-Havest losses every year.", "tokens": [50364, 31011, 18293, 2217, 4, 281, 3356, 4, 295, 1080, 1755, 281, 2183, 12, 39, 706, 377, 15352, 633, 1064, 13, 50628], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.2602535096725615, "compression_ratio": 1.5274261603375527, "no_speech_prob": 0.010404029861092567, "words": [{"word": " Kenya", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.38, "probability": 0.9668236970901489}, {"word": " loses", "start": 0.38, "end": 0.7, "probability": 0.9818056225776672}, {"word": " 30", "start": 0.7, "end": 1.16, "probability": 0.9720326662063599}, {"word": "%", "start": 1.16, "end": 1.32, "probability": 0.3173272907733917}, {"word": " to", "start": 1.32, "end": 1.32, "probability": 0.8805042505264282}, {"word": " 40", "start": 1.32, "end": 1.72, "probability": 0.9858653545379639}, {"word": "%", "start": 1.72, "end": 2.0, "probability": 0.9891487956047058}, {"word": " of", "start": 2.0, "end": 2.22, "probability": 0.9871585965156555}, {"word": " its", "start": 2.22, "end": 2.44, "probability": 0.9484993815422058}, {"word": " food", "start": 2.44, "end": 2.68, "probability": 0.9546677470207214}, {"word": " to", "start": 2.68, "end": 2.92, "probability": 0.9474181532859802}, {"word": " post", "start": 2.92, "end": 3.26, "probability": 0.9694628715515137}, {"word": "-Havest", "start": 3.26, "end": 3.58, "probability": 0.5693948268890381}, {"word": " losses", "start": 3.58, "end": 3.88, "probability": 0.9638211727142334}, {"word": " every", "start": 3.88, "end": 4.56, "probability": 0.94935142993927}, {"word": " year.", "start": 4.56, "end": 4.98, "probability": 0.9977903366088867}]}, {"id": 1, "seek": 0, "start": 5.46, "end": 9.64, "text": " Amaze farmer in 